 similar to lines we considered for p+q = -2? Actually p+q = (1 - q) + q = 1, so |p+q|=1. So these lines have sum change of 1 per step, but due to q>=2, the bound is floor((k+1)/2). So they can have up to 3 points. Let's test for q=3 (p=-2). Equation: (q-1) x + q y = 2x + 3y = d. Compute 2x+3y for points:\n\n(1,1):2+3=5\n(1,2):2+6=8\n(1,3):2+9=11\n(1,4):2+12=14\n(1,5):2+15=17\n(2,1):4+3=7\n(2,2):4+6=10\n(2,3):4+9=13\n(2,4):4+12=16\n(3,1):6+3=9\n(3,2):6+6=12\n(3,3):6+9=15\n(4,1):8+3=11\n(4,2):8+6=14\n(5,1):10+3=13\n\nNow find d with 3 points: d=11 appears at (1,3) and (4,1) => 2 points. d=14 appears at (1,4) and (4,2) => 2 points. d=13 appears at (2,3) and (5,1) => 2 points. d=8 appears only (1,2). d=7 only (2,1). d=5 only (1,1). d=9 only (3,1). d=10 only (2,2). d=12 only (3,2). d=15 only (3,3). d=16 only (2,4). d=17 only (1,5). So no triple.\n\nNow q=4 (p=-3). Equation: (q-1) x + q y = 3x + 4y = d. Compute 3x+4y:\n\n(1,1):3+4=7\n(1,2):3+8=11\n(1,3):3+12=15\n(1,4):3+16=19\n(1,5):3+20=23\n(2,1):6+4=10\n(2,2):6+8=14\n(2,3):6+12=18\n(2,4):6+16=22\n(3,1):9+4=13\n(3,2):9+8=17\n(3,3):9+12=21\n(4,1):12+4=16\n(4,2):12+8=20\n(5,1):15+4=19\n\nNow find d with 3 points: d=19 appears at (1,4) and (5,1) => 2 points. d=7 only (1,1). d=11 only (1,2). d=15 only (1,3). d=23 only (1,5). d=10 only (2,1). d=14 only (2,2). d=18 only (2,3). d=22 only (2,4). d=13 only (3,1). d=17 only (3,2). d=21 only (3,3). d=16 only (4,1). d=20 only (4,2). So no triple.\n\nThus lines with p+q=1 (i.e., slopes -1/2, -2/3, -3/4, ...) do not give triple points beyond the -1/2 case we already used. Actually -1/2 gave triple points (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). So that is one of them (q=2). So we have used that.\n\nNow p+q = -1: p = -1 - q. Since q>0, p negative. p cannot be -q (i.e., slope -1). So p = -1 - q != -q => -1 - q != -q => -1 != 0, always true. So allowed. So p+q = -1 => p = -1 - q. So p = -(q+1). So slope = p/q = -(q+1)/q = -1 - 1/q. For q=1 => p=-2 => slope -2 (already considered). For q=2 => p=-3 => slope -3/2. For q=3 => p=-4 => slope -4/3, etc. These lines have |p+q|=1, but q>=1. However q=1 yields p=-2, slope -2 (already considered). q=2 yields slope -3/2. Let's test if slope -3/2 lines can have 3 points.\n\nEquation: p x - q y = c => -(q+1) x - q y = c => (q+1) x + q y = -c => (q+1) x + q y = d. For q=2 => 3x + 2y = d. Compute 3x+2y for points:\n\n(1,1):3+2=5\n(1,2):3+4=7\n(1,3):3+6=9\n(1,4):3+8=11\n(1,5):3+10=13\n(2,1):6+2=8\n(2,2):6+4=10\n(2,3):6+6=12\n(2,4):6+8=14\n(3,1):9+2=11\n(3,2):9+4=13\n(3,3):9+6=15\n(4,1):12+2=14\n(4,2):12+4=16\n(5,1):15+2=17\n\nNow find d with 3 points: d=11 appears at (1,4) and (3,1) => 2 points. d=13 appears at (1,5) and (3,2) => 2 points. d=14 appears at (2,4) and (4,1) => 2 points. d=7 only (1,2). d=8 only (2,1). d=9 only (1,3). d=10 only (2,2). d=12 only (2,3). d=15 only (3,3). d=16 only (4,2). d=17 only (5,1). So no triple.\n\nFor q=3 => slope -4/3, equation 4x+3y = d. Compute 4x+3y:\n\n(1,1):4+3=7\n(1,2):4+6=10\n(1,3):4+9=13\n(1,4):4+12=16\n(1,5):4+15=19\n(2,1):8+3=11\n(2,2):8+6=14\n(2,3):8+9=17\n(2,4):8+12=20\n(3,1):12+3=15\n(3,2):12+6=18\n(3,3):12+9=21\n(4,1):16+3=19\n(4,2):16+6=22\n(5,1):20+3=23\n\nFind d with 3 points: d=19 appears at (1,5) and (4,1) => 2 points. d=7 only (1,1). d=10 only (1,2). d=13 only (1,3). d=16 only (1,4). d=11 only (2,1). d=14 only (2,2). d=17 only (2,3). d=20 only (2,4). d=15 only (3,1). d=18 only (3,2). d=21 only (3,3). d=22 only (4,2). d=23 only (5,1). So no triple.\n\nThus only slopes that give triple points are slope 1, -2, -1/2. So indeed only three lines can have 3 points in T_5. So we have three lines each with 3 points. But we need 5 lines each with 3 points. So impossible. So k=5 impossible.\n\nThus the solution's claim that only k=1 and 3 possible among odd k seems plausible.\n\nNow we need to verify that k=0 is possible: all vertical lines x=1,...,x=n. Indeed those are non-sunny lines, covering all points. So k=0 works.\n\nThus answer {0,1,3} appears correct.\n\nNow we need to verify the reasoning: The solution uses reduction to vertical lines, bound on points per sunny line, counting argument, classification of lines achieving bound, and concluding only k=1,3 possible. The reasoning seems valid. However we need to check if any step is flawed or missing justification.\n\nPotential issues:\n\n- The reduction step: \"Replace each non‑sunny line by a vertical line that passes through the leftmost column not yet occupied by a vertical line.\" This is plausible but we need to ensure that the replacement does not cause lines to intersect at same points causing duplication? Actually we only need to cover points, not avoid overlapping lines. Distinctness of lines: vertical lines at distinct x coordinates are distinct. The replacement ensures distinctness. Does the replacement preserve coverage of all points? For each non-sunny line, we replace it with a vertical line covering all points in a column. Since a non-sunny line can intersect each column at at most one point (if it's horizontal or slope -1), the vertical line will cover at least as many points in that column as the original line. However, the original line may have covered points in multiple columns (e.g., a horizontal line covers many points across columns). Replacing it with a vertical line covering a column may not cover points that were originally covered by that line in other columns. But those points may be covered by other lines (sunny lines or other non-sunny lines). The argument is that we can replace each non-sunny line one by one, ensuring that after each replacement, all points remain covered because we can choose the column that is not yet covered by any vertical line. However, we need to ensure that the points originally covered by the non-sunny line in other columns are still covered by other lines. Since we are replacing each non-sunny line with a vertical line that covers a column that previously had no vertical line, the points in that column that were previously covered by the original line are now covered by the new vertical line. Points in other columns that were covered by the original line may still be covered by other lines (sunny lines or other non-sunny lines). But we need to ensure that after all replacements, each column has at most one vertical line, and all points are covered. This is a known technique used in similar problems (like covering a triangular grid with lines). It seems valid.\n\n- The bound on points per sunny line: The solution uses integer representation p x - q y = c, with gcd(p,q)=1, q>0, p != 0, p != -q. Then they derive that the number of integer points in T_k on the line is at most floor((k+1)/2). The derivation uses the sum x+y changes by p+q each step. They consider two cases: |p+q| >= 2 and |p+q| = 1. For |p+q| >= 2, they bound number of points by floor((k-1)/|p+q|)+1 <= floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). For |p+q| = 1, they argue q >= 2 (since p=0 would be horizontal) and then bound number of points by floor(k/q)+1 <= floor((k+1)/2). This seems correct.\n\n- Counting argument: They compute total points in T_k = k(k+1)/2. Then they require k * floor((k+1)/2) >= k(k+1)/2. This yields condition that k must be odd. For even k, inequality fails. For odd k, equality holds. So each sunny line must contain exactly floor((k+1)/2) points, i.e., (k+1)/2 points, and lines must be pairwise disjoint. This is correct.\n\n- Classification of lines achieving bound: They claim that only three possibilities for p,q with |p+q|=2 produce lines that can achieve the bound. They list p=q=1 (slope 1), p=-2,q=1 (slope -2), p=-1,q=2 (slope -1/2). They also consider |p+q|=1 case with q>=2, but they argue that bound is still floor((k+1)/2) and equality can be achieved only if q=2 (since floor(k/q)+1 = floor((k+1)/2) requires q=2). Let's verify: For odd k = 2m+1, floor(k/q)+1 = floor((2m+1)/q)+1. To equal m+1, we need floor((2m+1)/q) = m. This holds if q=2 (since floor((2m+1)/2) = m). For q>2, floor((2m+1)/q) <= floor((2m+1)/3) <= m-1 for m>=1? Let's check: For m=1 (k=3), floor(3/3)=1 = m? Actually m=1, floor(3/3)=1 = m, so q=3 also works for k=3? Let's test: k=3, q=3 => floor(3/3)=1 => +1 =2 = m+1 = 2. So q=3 also yields equality for k=3. But for larger k, q>2 yields floor((2m+1)/q) m for m>=2? Let's test m=2 (k=5), floor(5/3)=1 2, so fails. So for k>3, only q=2 works. So the only lines with |p+q|=1 that achieve bound for k>3 are those with q=2, p = -1 (since p = 1 - q = -1). That's slope -1/2, which is already included. So classification seems correct.\n\n- Then they argue that to cover T_{2m+1} with 2m+1 lines, we must use only the three lines (10). But we need to verify that for k=1 (m=0) and k=3 (m=1), we can indeed use only those three lines. For k=1, we only need one line, any sunny line works, but they claim we need to use only those three lines? Actually for k=1, we can use any sunny line passing through the single point (1,1). The three lines include y=x (which passes through (1,1)). So it's fine.\n\nFor k=3, we need three lines each with 2 points. The three lines listed indeed work. So the classification holds.\n\n- For k>3 odd, they claim we need to use only those three lines, but we have more lines than three, which is impossible. However, we need to verify that there is no other set of lines (maybe using some lines with |p+q|=1 and q=2, but we already have those slopes -1/2, -2, 1). Actually those three slopes correspond to p+q = 2 (p=q=1) and p+q = -2 (p=-2,q=1 and p=-1,q=2). So there are exactly three slopes that achieve the bound. So any line achieving the bound must be one of those three slopes. So any covering must use only those three slopes. Since each line can be placed at different intercepts (different c values), but the bound is achieved only when the line passes through exactly m+1 points of T_{2m+1}. For each slope, there may be multiple lines with that slope that intersect T_{2m+1} in m+1 points. However, the solution claims that for each slope, there is exactly one line that contains m+1 points (the one that passes through the appropriate points). Let's verify for k=5 (m=2). For slope 1 (p=q=1), lines x-y = c. For c=0, we have points (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) => 3 points. For c=1, points (2,1),(3,2),(4,3?) but (4,3) not in T_5 because 4+3=7 >6. So only two points. For c=-1, points (1,2),(2,3),(3,4?) (3,4) not in T_5 because 3+4=7 >6. So only two points. So only c=0 yields 3 points. So indeed only one line of slope 1 works.\n\nFor slope -2 (2x + y = d). For d=7 we got three points (1,5),(2,3),(3,1). For other d values, we got at most 2 points. So only one line works.\n\nFor slope -1/2 (x + 2y = d). For d=7 we got three points (1,3),(3,2),(5,1). For other d values, at most 2 points. So only one line works.\n\nThus for k=5, there are exactly three lines that achieve bound, each with 3 points. So we cannot have 5 lines each with 3 points. So impossible.\n\nFor larger odd k, similar pattern: each slope yields exactly one line with m+1 points. So we have at most three lines achieving bound, but need 2m+1 lines. So impossible for m>=2 (k>=5). So only k=1,3 possible.\n\nThus reasoning seems valid.\n\nPotential issues: The solution's step \"Thus, to cover T_{2m+1} with 2m+1 sunny lines we must use only the three lines (10). Hence we need 2m+1 <= 3; i.e., k=1 or k=3.\" This is a bit abrupt: they assume that each sunny line must achieve the bound (i.e., contain exactly m+1 points). This is true because equality holds in the counting inequality. However, they need to argue that if any line contains fewer points, then some other line must contain more points to compensate, which is impossible because bound is maximum. So all lines must achieve the bound. This is correct.\n\nThus reasoning is valid.\n\nNow we need to check presentation issues: Are there any gaps or missing justifications that could be considered \"Justification Gap\"? Possibly the reduction step could be considered insufficiently justified: they claim we can replace each non-sunny line by a vertical line covering the leftmost column not yet occupied, and that this does not affect coverage. This is plausible but not fully proven. However, it's a standard argument; we can consider it a minor justification gap. Also the classification of lines achieving bound may be a bit terse: they claim \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1, p=-2,q=1, p=-1,q=2.\" They didn't justify why these are the only possibilities for |p+q|=2 with p != 0, p != -q, gcd(p,q)=1, q>0. Actually there are more possibilities: e.g., p=2,q=0 (vertical) not allowed; p=0,q=2 (horizontal) not allowed; p=-3,q=5 gives p+q=2? Wait -3+5=2, gcd(-3,5)=1, q>0, p != 0, p != -q (p != -5). So p=-3,q=5 is allowed and yields slope -3/5. But does that line achieve the bound? They claim only three possibilities. Actually we need to examine: For |p+q|=2, we have p+q = 2 or p+q = -2. For p+q = 2, we have p = 2 - q. Since q>0, p can be any integer such that p+q=2. So possibilities: (p,q) = (1,1), (0,2), (-1,3), (-2,4), ... etc. But p cannot be 0 (horizontal) and p cannot be -q (slope -1). So we need p != 0, p != -q. For q=2, p=0 (excluded). For q=3, p=-1 (allowed). For q=4, p=-2 (allowed). For q=5, p=-3 (allowed). So there are infinitely many possibilities. However, they also require gcd(p,q)=1. For q=3, p=-1, gcd(1,3)=1, okay. For q=4, p=-2, gcd(2,4)=2, not allowed (must be 1). So (p,q)=(-2,4) not allowed. For q=5, p=-3, gcd(3,5)=1, allowed. So there are many possibilities. But do these lines achieve the bound? Let's test for k=5 (m=2). For p=-1,q=3 (p+q=2). This corresponds to line -x - 3y = c => x + 3y = -c => x + 3y = d. We earlier computed that for T_5, lines x+3y = d have at most 2 points. So they do not achieve bound. So the solution's claim that only three possibilities achieve bound is correct, but they didn't justify why other possibilities cannot achieve bound. They gave a brief argument: \"Equation (2.1) with |p+q|=2 gives three possibilities: p=q=1 (slope 1); p=-2,q=