nWe need to cover points in columns 2-5. Let's denote the set S = {(2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(4,1),(4,2),(5,1)}.\n\nWe need 4 lines, each line can be any line not vertical/horizontal/slope -1. Each line can contain any number of points from S, but must be distinct lines. We need to cover all points.\n\nWe can try to find a covering using a computer search, but we can reason manually.\n\nObservation: Points in S have coordinates (i,j) with i≥2, j≥1, i+j ≤6 (since n=5). So the points are:\n\n- (2,1) sum=3\n- (2,2) sum=4\n- (2,3) sum=5\n- (2,4) sum=6\n- (3,1) sum=4\n- (3,2) sum=5\n- (3,3) sum=6\n- (4,1) sum=5\n- (4,2) sum=6\n- (5,1) sum=6\n\nWe need to cover these with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe can try to find lines that go through three points each, maybe covering all 10 points with 4 lines each covering 2-3 points.\n\nLet's consider lines of slope 1 (45-degree). Points with slope 1 lines: line through (2,1) and (3,2) slope 1, also passes through (4,3) not in S, (5,4) not in S. So L1: (2,1)-(3,2). Could also include (5,4) not in S. So L1 covers 2 points.\n\nLine through (2,2) and (3,3) slope 1, also passes through (4,4) not in S. So L2: (2,2)-(3,3). Could also include (5,5) not in S. So L2 covers 2 points.\n\nLine through (2,3) and (3,4) not in S. So slope 1 lines only cover pairs.\n\nLines of slope -2: line through (2,4) and (3,1) slope (1-4)/(3-2) = -3, not -2. Actually slope -3. Let's compute slopes for various pairs.\n\nWe can try to find lines that cover three points. For three points to be collinear, they must satisfy linear equation. Let's see if any three points in S are collinear with a slope not 0, infinite, -1.\n\nCheck if any three points share same slope and intercept.\n\nWe can try to find lines that pass through points (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) but (4,3) not in S. So no triple.\n\nCheck line through (2,1),(4,2): slope (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2 = 0.5, passes through (3,1.5) not integer. So only two points.\n\nCheck line through (2,1),(5,1): horizontal, not al